course: dictionary
lesson: 3524
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The dictionary gives three meanings for fawn, so you must use the context to decide which one fits. Context means the words and sentences around the word. «A mother deer hides her fawn for hours while she feeds nearby.» Hiding and feeding tell you that a fawn is an animal here: a young deer.
explain: Colour words can also be tricky. Read this: «Her new coat was a soft fawn, the colour of sand.» The words soft and colour show that fawn names a shade, a light yellowish-brown, and not an animal. Always ask: what word would fit here? Then swap your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: Fawn can also be a verb, an action word. «He would fawn over the teacher, praising everything she said.» Praising too much, and trying to get someone to like you, is the meaning here. Notice that the same spelling can do three different jobs, so the clues around it matter most.
fact: A word with more than one meaning is called a word with multiple meanings.
fact: Context clues, such as the words around the word, help you choose the right meaning.
fact: «Fawn» (noun) means a young deer, usually less than a year old, often with white spots.
fact: «Fawn» (adjective) means a light yellowish-brown colour.
fact: «To fawn over someone» means to praise and please them too much, to get them to like you.
exercise: «A mother deer hides her fawn for hours while she feeds nearby.» What is a fawn in this sentence? | A person who praises too much | A light yellowish-brown colour | *A young deer | A path through the forest
exercise: «Her new coat was a soft fawn, the colour of sand.» What does fawn mean here? | To run quickly through the grass | To praise someone too much | A young deer | *A light yellowish-brown colour
exercise: «The shop assistant kept fawning over the rich customer, praising everything he said.» What does fawning mean? | To hide in long grass | To feed nearby | *To praise and please someone too much | To run away quickly
test:
«Imagine walking through long grass and finding a spotted fawn lying perfectly still.» What should you do with a fawn found alone? | Pick it up and take it home | *Leave it alone, because its mother is usually nearby | Touch it to see if it is warm | Shout loudly to wake it up
«A baby deer, called a fawn, is often left hidden in the grass.» Which clue tells you that fawn is a young deer? | *The words baby deer, which name the same animal | The word grass, which is a colour | The word hidden, which means to praise | The word left, which means to run away
exam: Which sentence uses fawn to mean praise too much? | *«He would fawn over the teacher to get her to like him.» | «Her coat was a pale fawn.» | «The fawn lay still in the long grass.» | «The fawn was born in the spring.»
